        <h2>Method 1: A Browser-Based Paste Tool</h2>

        <p>Open <a href="https://pastedownloadapp.com/">pastedownloadapp.com</a>, press Ctrl+V (or Cmd+V on Mac), and the clipboard image appears ready to download in PNG, JPEG, or WebP. No sign-up, no size limit, and nothing is uploaded: the conversion happens with the browser's own Canvas API, entirely on your device.</p>

        <figure class="my-6 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft">
            <img src="/blog/screenshots/paste-tool-chart-20-9kb-result.webp" alt="Screenshot of PasteDownloadApp after pasting a 1000 by 640 test chart, showing a PNG output of 20.9 KB" width="1200" height="1179" class="w-full max-w-lg mx-auto" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </figure>

        <p>That screenshot is a real run: a 1000&times;640 test chart, pasted and converted to a 20.9 KB PNG, reported directly by the tool. This is the only method of the five that gives you a choice of output format at full resolution with nothing sent anywhere.</p>

        <h2>Method 2: Paste Into an Email or Document</h2>

        <p>Open a compose window or a Google Doc and paste. Right-click the result and choose Save Image or Download. It works if you are already in one of those apps, but it comes with a real cost most guides skip: Gmail resizes inline pasted images for the "small" or "best fit" display setting in the composer, so what you save back out is not the original resolution, only a true file attachment is. Google Docs has no built-in compressor and normally keeps images at their original size, but it caps embedded images at roughly 2000&times;2000 pixels; paste something larger and Docs downscales it on the way in, silently. If you specifically need a real attachment rather than inline content, see <a href="/blog/paste-screenshot-into-email-attachment/">how to turn a pasted screenshot into an email attachment</a>.</p>

        <h2>Method 3: An Online Image Editor</h2>

        <p>Some image editing sites accept a clipboard paste into their canvas and expose an export or download button. It is the same underlying mechanic as Method 1, just wrapped inside a larger editing suite, and useful specifically when you also want to crop or resize before saving. The tradeoff is that these sites vary widely in whether they process client-side or upload your image to a server first; check before pasting anything sensitive.</p>

        <h2>Method 4: Native OS Tools</h2>

        <p>Each desktop OS has a built-in way to turn a clipboard image into a file, and they are not interchangeable:</p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Windows:</strong> open Paint, paste with Ctrl+V, then File &gt; Save As and pick a format.</li>
            <li><strong>Mac:</strong> open Preview, use File &gt; New from Clipboard (or Cmd+N from Spotlight), then File &gt; Export to choose the format explicitly. For the full breakdown, including why a plain Save can produce an oversized file, see <a href="/blog/download-copied-image-mac/">copying and saving a picture on Mac</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Linux:</strong> open GIMP, use Edit &gt; Paste as &gt; New Image (Shift+Ctrl+V), then File &gt; Export As and choose PNG or another format.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>All three work, but they are OS-specific, need an app you may not have open already, and involve more menu navigation than a browser tab you can leave pinned. If your clipboard image originated in a specific browser, it is also worth knowing that <a href="/blog/clipboard-paste-chrome-firefox-safari/">Chrome, Firefox, and Safari do not implement clipboard image access identically</a>.</p>

